WEBVTT
TITLE Sequencing And Order Of Evaluation In C — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Precedence decides which operator takes which operand. It says nothing about which operand is computed first.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The tree is fixed by the grammar. The schedule is not fixed by anything in the source text.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Three schedules, one tree, one value: g then h then f, or f then g then h, or h then g then f.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
So two questions: grouping, answered by precedence and associativity; order of evaluation, answered by the standard as unspecified.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Evaluating i++ does two things: it computes the value 5, and it stores 6. Only the store is a side effect.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
For i++ the value computation comes first and the store after: that is why it yields the old value.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
An assignment is also two events: the value computations of both sides, then the store to the left side.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Every question on this topic is a question about these small events and which of them may be reordered.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A sequence point is a barrier: everything before it is complete, including side effects, before anything after it begins.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The barriers that decide expression questions: the call, &&, ||, the comma operator, ?:, and the end of a full expression.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A full expression ends at the semicolon, the condition of if or while, each part of a for header, or the expression in return.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Inside a segment nothing is ordered. That gap is where every undefined answer in this lesson lives.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The rule: if a side effect on a scalar object is unsequenced with another side effect on it, or with a read of it, the behaviour is undefined.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Undefined is not unknown. It is the absence of any requirement: the compiler may do anything at all.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
i = i++ + 1 has two stores to i and no barrier between them, so the whole statement is undefined.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
And the answer still comes out as 6 on every ordering. One answer does not make the statement defined.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Take i equal to 5 and the expression i++ + i++. Four events: read, store, read, store.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Six orderings are legal. Two of them give 11, and the other four give 10.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So the set of answers is 10 and 11, and three pairs of events are unsequenced: each read against the other store, and the two stores against each other.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
A question that asks for the value of i++ + i++ has no answer to mark. The defensible answer is that the behaviour is undefined.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
One answer with free order: f() + g(). Two orderings, the same sum, and the program is well defined.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Several answers: f(i++, i). Three orderings give the argument pairs 5,5 and 5,6, so the call may see either.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
One answer with fixed order: x = i + 1. One ordering, one value, defined.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Three verdicts: defined with one answer, defined with an order the standard leaves open, and undefined.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
i < n && a[i] == x is safe because && puts a barrier after its first operand.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Written the other way round, a[i] == x && i < n, the read of a[i] happens before the guard, and the guard protects nothing.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The comma operator and ?: also carry a barrier. The comma between arguments does not.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
So a barrier can be bought with a comma operator, and that is the usual repair: x = (i++, i) has one ordering and one answer.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Four shapes, four verdicts. i++ + i++: undefined, two answers, and no rewrite is needed because nobody wants it.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
a[i] = i++: undefined, because the store to i is unsequenced with the read of i used as the index.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
f(i++, i): undefined, because the two arguments are unsequenced and one writes what the other reads.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
i = i++ + 1: undefined with a single answer of 6. That is the one that teaches the rule, because the answer looks right.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Step one: find the full expression. Step two: mark the barriers inside it.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Step three: list every access to each variable in the segment, and mark which are writes.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Step four: if two of them are unsequenced and at least one is a write, stop. The answer is undefined.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Step five, only if no conflict: the answer is fixed, and the only freedom left is the order of calls that touch nothing shared.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The chain: an expression is events; barriers order the events; a gap lets them reorder; two unsequenced accesses with a write are undefined.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Six slips: reading associativity as execution, calling unspecified undefined, forgetting the argument comma is not an operator, trusting one printed answer, and two more.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
The one-line test: name the barriers, then ask whether anything in the gap writes what anything else in the gap reads.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The revision sheet keeps the three verdicts and the four rewrites; the practice set keeps the arithmetic of the enumeration.

